>47 record highs</strong>, fewer than the 78 that chance alone would give. The 1920s hold the most record highs of any decade (64). Columbus' all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on September 5, 1925.","t_chg":"Columbus' nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 117 complete years: Columbus' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.07 to +0.23), about +1.8\u00b0F since 1903. Columbus' average high shows no clear change: \u22120.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.14 to +0.01) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.53\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.23 to +0.83) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.41\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.08 to +0.75). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; 5 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Columbus' yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.14 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 48.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 2009 (80.20 in); the driest, 1999 (26.39 in). Columbus almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.6 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}